<%= colour_palette('maps') %>

## Using colour combinations on maps

- Use as few colours as the map needs. Every extra colour is one more thing to look up in the key.
- Give data on the map, such as a zone or a marker, at least 3:1 contrast with the base map. If it falls short, add an outline, simplify the map or give the same facts in a table.
- Give each marker a shape, letter or label as well as a colour, so that people who cannot tell the colours apart can still read the map.
- Give every map a key that names each colour, shape and line style.
- Do not let the colour of an area suggest a political party.
- Test the map with the people who will use it.

[Test the real colour pairs](colour.html#bg-col-03) explains the contrast that text and controls need.

### Example

Three Home Assistant zones, Home, Work and School, drawn on a base map, as they might appear on a custom map card. Notice that every zone uses the same colour with a darker outline, and that each zone is named, so you can tell them apart without colour. The coastline and zones are invented.

<%= colour_example('map-govuk-zones', layout: :map) %>

### Colours used in example

#### Points of interest

Zones and markers are Accent green with a Primary green outline. Accent green on its own is too pale against the land, so the outline gives the 3:1 contrast. Labels, such as zone names, are Black.

<%= colour_swatches(%w[green-accent green black], key: 'example') %>

#### Base map

Land is Black tint 95% with a Black tint 80% outline. Sea is Teal tint 95%, with a band of Teal tint 80% along the coast.

<%= colour_swatches(%w[black-tint-80 black-tint-95 teal-tint-80 teal-tint-95], key: 'example') %>

## Working OpenStreetMap example

The same colours on real OpenStreetMap data around Cardiff Bay, with 2 invented device locations. Nothing is requested from OpenStreetMap until you select **Load OpenStreetMap**.

When the map loads, notice that:

- roads are White with Black tint 80% edges
- buildings, railways and boundaries are Black tint 80%
- rivers and other narrow water are Teal tint 80%, so they stay visible against the land, and the coastal band also edges lakes
- each device marker has its own shape and letter as well as its colour

<%= example('map-neighbourhood-records') %>

## In Home Assistant

Use these colours for maps you design, such as a custom map card or a picture of a map in a guide. civica does not restyle the native Map card, which keeps its own map styles. Keep the map provider's attribution visible on any map.

[Maps, zones and location records](../home-assistant/maps-and-zones.html) explains how to show locations, uncertainty and missing records.
